OK, we have two wonderful rebuttals to my 'why 7?' argument. We have the 
'Devteam chose 7 so it must be right' answer and the '3 makes sense, so 
therefore 7' answer. Not exactly fantastic reasoning in either case. It 
3 makes sense, why not go with 3? For instance, disallowing recharging 
or by giving them 1 standard charge, recharging back up to 1 and then 
wresting the last 1. I think that the DevTeam have not fully thought 
throught the idea of 7 wishes (can they give me a reason?) and kind of 
had it forced upon them by the mechanics after deciding on 3 initial 
wishes. I think they just haven't thought about it much since then.

As an aside, if people think that this makes the game too hard, I would 
be quite happy to balance it by some other change to gameplay that makes 
it a little easier (I am not suggesting any here though). I don't want 
it to be harder, just more balanced.

Adding chance to a game has two basic effects, on one hand, it makes the 
game play differently each time, leading to replayability. On the other, 
it makes some instances of playing the game easier than other ones. I 
think that the first of these two is the most important (indeed, quite 
critical for a one player game), while the other can be problematic. 
Ideally (I think) the game will play out differently each time, leading 
to new and interesting tactics and strategies being required, but be as 
close as possible to the ideal level of challenge. The change to early 
WoWs would keep us closer to whatever level of challenge is set and 
prevent rare and big fluctuations towards a lower level of challenge. 
Gnomes with WoDs do the reverse. Assuming that the base level of 
challenge is set appropriately, these big fluctuations make the 
challenge less ideal and are a bad thing. 

